(ASK YOUR PDFs | A FIELD GUIDE TO RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION) Tj
T*
(An original sample document for the Folio demo collection) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Why retrieval matters) Tj
T*
(A language model can write fluent answers, but fluency does not guarantee that an answer ) Tj
T*
(reflects a particular book, report, or policy. Retrieval-augmented generation \(RAG\) connects a ) Tj
T*
(language model to a collection of source documents. The system searches for relevant passages, ) Tj
T*
(supplies those passages with the user's question, and asks the model to answer from that ) Tj
T*
(evidence. This separates finding evidence from composing a response.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A useful RAG answer is traceable. Each retrieved passage should preserve the document name and ) Tj
T*
(page number from which its text came. A citation such as [02-embedding-notes.pdf, p. 2] lets a ) Tj
T*
(reader return to the original page and check the context. Citations are metadata carried through ) Tj
T*
(the pipeline; the model should not invent them.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. From PDF to searchable passages) Tj
T*
(PDFs are designed to preserve layout, not necessarily to expose clean reading order. A ) Tj
T*
(text-based PDF often contains extractable words, while a scanned page may contain only an image. ) Tj
T*
(Scanned files need optical character recognition before ordinary text retrieval can work. ) Tj
T*
(Extraction quality should be checked, especially for columns, footnotes, tables, and unusual ) Tj
T*
(fonts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(After extraction, split text into chunks. A chunk should be long enough to contain a complete ) Tj
T*
(idea, but short enough that a search result stays focused. A practical starting point is several ) Tj
T*
(hundred words, then adjust based on the corpus and embedding model. Split near paragraph or ) Tj
T*
(sentence boundaries when possible. A small overlap between neighboring chunks reduces the chance ) Tj
T*
(of cutting an important definition or argument in half.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Keep page boundaries explicit. When a chunk belongs to a single PDF page, storing that page ) Tj
T*
(number makes citations direct and reliable. If chunks span pages, store the first and last page ) Tj
T*
(or create page-level chunks. Preserve the source filename, page, and a stable chunk identifier ) Tj
T*
(alongside the text.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Embeddings and retrieval) Tj
T*
(An embedding model maps a passage to a vector of numbers that represents semantic features. ) Tj
T*
(Questions and passages encoded by the same model can be compared in vector space. Similarity ) Tj
T*
(search can find a passage that discusses the same idea even when it uses different wording from ) Tj
T*
(the question. This is why semantic retrieval complements exact keyword search.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A vector database stores embeddings and associated metadata. At query time, encode the question, ) Tj
T*
(request the nearest vectors, and return their text plus metadata. Cosine similarity is a common ) Tj
T*
(comparison measure. The number of requested passages, often called top-k, controls the breadth ) Tj
T*
(of evidence supplied to generation. A very small k can omit a useful detail. A very large k can ) Tj
T*
(introduce unrelated passages and consume more context.) Tj
T*
0 -10 Td
