() Tj
T*
(4. Grounded generation and uncertainty) Tj
T*
(The generation prompt should say plainly that the retrieved excerpts are the only evidence the ) Tj
T*
(model may use. Ask it to cite claims using the supplied filename and page. If evidence is absent ) Tj
T*
(or inadequate, the answer should say: ?I don?t know based on the provided documents.? This ) Tj
T*
(response is more useful than a confident guess.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Retrieval similarity is not proof that a passage supports an answer. A high score can still ) Tj
T*
(retrieve a passage that shares vocabulary but addresses a different issue. Applications should ) Tj
T*
(inspect source quality, test representative questions, and allow the model to decline when ) Tj
T*
(context is insufficient. The user should be able to see the cited sources, not just the polished ) Tj
T*
(answer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. A practical quality checklist) Tj
T*
(Check extraction on short and long pages. Confirm that chunks retain their filename and correct ) Tj
T*
(page. Re-index changed files instead of keeping stale chunks. Try questions with direct answers, ) Tj
T*
(paraphrased questions, multi-part questions, and questions whose answers are absent. Verify each ) Tj
T*
(citation against the original PDF. Track failures such as missing context, inaccurate page ) Tj
T*
(numbers, weak OCR, and overly broad chunks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A small, transparent RAG system can be useful without claiming that retrieval eliminates errors. ) Tj
T*
(Its value comes from making source material easier to find while keeping the evidence visible. ) Tj
T*
(Good chunking, careful metadata, constrained generation, and honest uncertainty work together; ) Tj
T*
(none is a substitute for checking the cited page.) Tj
T*
